## Methods

A method is a function attached to a type. It is declared with a receiver: a parameter written between the `func` keyword and the method name.

In the example, the receiver `(r Rectangle)` attaches the method `Area` to `Rectangle`. Inside the method, the fields are accessed with a dot, as in `r.Width`. The method is called with the same notation: `r.Area()`.

The receiver is similar to `self` in Python. By convention, its name is a [short abbreviation](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments#receiver-names) of the type, such as `r` for `Rectangle`.

